<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/electrostatics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Two kinds of charge exist — positive and negative — and like kinds repel while opposites
    attract, with a force obeying the same inverse-square law as gravity but about
    <strong>10³⁶ times stronger</strong>. Rub a balloon on your hair: electrons transfer, the
    balloon gains net negative charge, and Coulomb's law does the rest. Electrostatics is
    charge at rest — the physics before the wires — and it is not a curiosity: every atom is
    held together by it, every lightning bolt discharges it, and every photocopier and paint
    sprayer engineers it on purpose. This chapter builds the concepts (charge, field,
    potential) that the circuits, magnetism and atomic chapters all spend.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Coulomb's law for two microcoulomb charges — halve the distance, quadruple the force" widget="plotter" config={coulombCfg} />

  <p>
    F = kq₁q₂/r² with k = 8.99 × 10⁹ N·m²/C². Slide from 1 m to 10 cm (one tenth) and the force
    grows a hundredfold. That steepness is why static shocks are violent up close and nothing
    across the room.
  </p>

  <h2>Charge: what it is and how objects get it</h2>
  <p>
    Charge is a property of matter, carried by protons (positive) and electrons (negative),
    measured in coulombs. The basic unit is the electron's charge, <strong>e = 1.602 ×
    10⁻¹⁹ C</strong> — all charge comes in whole multiples of it (charge is
    <em>quantised</em>), and the total is <strong>conserved</strong>: no process creates or
    destroys net charge, it only separates it. That last clause is the key to every "static
    electricity" demo: <em>rubbing never creates charge; it transfers it</em>. Rubbing the
    balloon on your hair peels electrons off the hair (which goes positive) onto the balloon
    (negative); the pair's total is unchanged. A charged balloon carries perhaps 10⁻⁸ C —
    about 60 billion electrons — a vanishingly small fraction of its total, yet enough to
    hold it to the ceiling. Charging happens two ways in the lab: by <strong>contact</strong>
    (touch a charged rod to a sphere and charge conducts across) and by
    <strong>induction</strong> — bring a charged rod near a neutral sphere, whose electrons
    pile up on the near side; earth the far side, remove the earth, then the rod, and the
    sphere is left with net charge opposite to the rod, <em>without ever touching it</em>.
    (Induction, not contact, is how lightning charges rooftops from a distance.)
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> in solids the positive charges move. They do not — protons
  are locked in nuclei, and practically nothing nuclear happens in a wire or a rubbed rod.
  Electrons do the moving, and the "positive direction" of conventional current is an 18th-century
  convention we kept. Also: a charged balloon sticks to a <em>neutral</em> wall — its nearby
  field shifts the wall's electrons slightly, inducing a surface charge that is closer and
  opposite, so attraction wins. Neutral objects are not immune; they are persuadable.</p>

  <h2>Coulomb's law, with numbers</h2>
  <p>
    The force between point charges is <strong>F = kq₁q₂/r²</strong>, k = 8.99 × 10⁹
    N·m²/C² — the electric twin of Newton's gravitation, with charge in the numerator where
    mass sits and repulsion where attraction defaulted. Worked example: two charges of
    1 µC (a hefty static charge) at 10 cm apart: F = 8.99 × 10⁹ × 10⁻⁶ × 10⁻⁶ / (0.1)² =
    <strong>0.9 N</strong> — about the weight of a 90 g apple, from two specks of excess
    charge. At 1 m the same pair manage 0.009 N. The inverse square is unforgiving: halve the
    separation, quadruple the force — the widget's curve — which is why "touching" changes
    everything and "nearby" changes little. And the comparison that rearranges your intuition:
    in a hydrogen atom, the electric pull between proton and electron exceeds their
    gravitational pull by roughly 10³⁹. Electricity is not a small effect that occasionally
    gets big; matter is <em>held together</em> by it, and gravity only rules once charges
    cancel — the cosmic-scale fact that stars and planets are neutral. Keep one calibration:
    a whole microcoulomb is a <em>large</em> static charge, and ordinary rubbed objects sit
    far below it — which makes these forces all the more impressive.
  </p>

  <h2>The field: replacing spooky action with local cause</h2>
  <p>
    Rather than say each charge "acts on" distant charges directly, physics interposes a
    <strong>field</strong>: every charge fills space with an electric field, and the force on
    any charge is its charge times the field <em>at its own location</em>. Formally,
    <strong>E = F/q</strong> — newtons per coulomb, the force a +1 C test charge would feel.
    The field concept earns its keep in pictures: <strong>field lines</strong> exit positive
    charges, enter negative ones, never cross, and crowd together where the field is strong.
    Between two parallel plates the lines are straight and evenly spaced — a
    <strong>uniform field</strong>, the geometry inside oscilloscopes and inkjet printers,
    with strength E = V/d (volts per gap-metre: 5,000 V across 1 cm is 500,000 N/C —
    air's breakdown is around 3 × 10⁶ V/m, the number that decides when a spark jumps).
    For a point charge the field thins as 1/r², mirroring the force law. The field is also
    the right language for shielding: in a hollow <strong>conductor</strong>, free electrons
    rearrange until the interior field is exactly zero — a <strong>Faraday cage</strong>,
    which is why a car (roughly) protects you from lightning and why a microwave's mesh door
    keeps 12 cm microwaves in while admitting visible light.
  </p>
  <p>
    Closely related is <strong>electric potential</strong>, V — the electric potential energy
    per unit charge at a point, measured in volts. Fields push; potentials score. Charge
    always "falls" from high to low potential (positive charges, that is), and the energy
    released is q·ΔV — the bookkeeping the circuits chapter runs with batteries. One
    memorable unit: the <strong>electron-volt</strong>, the energy a single electron gains
    crossing 1 V — 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J — the particle physics world's pocket change.
  </p>

  <h2>Three demonstrations, decoded</h2>
  <p>
    Run the field idea over three classic kitchen demos. <strong>The bending water
    stream:</strong> a charged rod held near a thin tap flow deflects it — water molecules are
    polar (hydrogen side slightly positive), so the rod attracts their near ends and repels
    their far ends; the near side wins because it is closer, a net attraction born of
    asymmetry. <strong>The balloon on the ceiling:</strong> charged rubber repels the
    ceiling's electrons, inducing a positive surface layer that binds it — induction at
    domestic scale. <strong>The crackle of pulled-off cling film:</strong> separating
    surfaces strip electrons from each other unevenly; the film's separate regions then
    attract and repel the rest of the roll — the same charge separation as the balloon,
    accelerated. Each demo is a textbook diagram with props, and each one is testable: do
    them under humid conditions and watch the effects weaken as leakage improves.
  </p>

  <h2>Conductors, insulators, and the engineering of charge</h2>
  <p>
    Materials divide by what their electrons may do. <strong>Conductors</strong> (metals)
    hold one or more electrons per atom loosely; the "electron sea" of the bonding chapter
    migrates freely, so charge placed anywhere spreads over the whole surface until mutual
    repulsion balances — and sharp points, where like-charges crowd, leak charge most
    easily (the physics of lightning conductors: the pointed rod bleeds charge quietly and
    offers the strike a preferred, earthed path). <strong>Insulators</strong> (plastic,
    glass, dry air) hold electrons tight; charge stays where it is put — which is why a
    rubbed rod holds its charge and why insulators charge by friction at all.
    <strong>Semiconductors</strong> sit in between, and their conductivity can be switched
    by impurities and fields — the controlled half-way house that transistors exploit
    (the computing chapters take it from there). <strong>Earthing</strong> is the universal
    tiebreaker: connect any conductor to the planet (a colossal reservoir) and excess charge
    drains to neutrality. Fuel tankers earth themselves before pumping because the flowing
    fuel's friction charging can build enough potential to spark — the reason every petrol
    station nozzle and aircraft fuelling rig has a bonding cable.
  </p>

  <h2>Electrostatics at work</h2>
  <p>
    The applications are quieter than lightning but everywhere. <strong>Laser printers and
    photocopiers</strong> charge a drum uniformly, then erase charge where light strikes;
    toner powder, charged oppositely, sticks only to the remaining image and is fused to
    paper — Coulomb's law as an office appliance. <strong>Electrostatic precipitators</strong>
    charge smoke particles and collect them on plates, scrubbing power-station flues.
    <strong>Electrostatic spray painting</strong> charges droplets so they repel each other
    into a fine mist and then wrap around earthed targets, cutting paint waste sharply.
    <strong>Van de Graaff generators</strong> accumulate charge on a dome until hair-raising
    potentials (hundreds of kilovolts) demonstrate the ideas at party scale. And
    <strong>lightning</strong> is the grand demo: charge separation inside storm clouds
    (ice and graupel colliding) builds fields until air breaks down — a typical flash
    carries tens of thousands of amperes and a hundred million volts, heating the channel
    to about 30,000 K (five times the Sun's surface), and the thunder is that channel's
    explosive expansion. The safety rules (avoid ridgelines, trees, umbrellas; crouch low;
    cars are safe because of the cage, not the rubber tyres) all read as applications of
    this page.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Humidity is the reason "static" is seasonal.</strong> Water molecules coat
    surfaces in humid air and give accumulated charge an easy leak to ground; in dry winter
    air (or inside a heated office) insulators hold charge for minutes, and every doorknob
    is a tiny thunderstorm. The effect is measurable: walking across carpet can charge a
    body to several kilovolts — the shock you feel is real (a few millijoules, harmless to
    you, occasionally fatal to a microchip, which is why electronics factories have
    wrist-straps and conductive floors).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>How the laws were pinned down.</strong> Coulomb's 1785 torsion balance measured
    the inverse square by twisting a silver wire against electric force — an exquisitely
    sensitive method that modern versions have pushed to extraordinary precision: the
    exponent in 1/r² is now known to agree with 2 to better than one part in 10¹⁶, because
    any deviation would let light "leak" charge — a bound set by watching photons from
    distant stars. Charge quantisation itself was established by Millikan's oil-drop
    experiment (1909): suspended droplets between charged plates, balancing gravity against
    electric force, revealed charges always in multiples of one number — e. Two of
    physics's foundational constants were measured with static electricity and patience.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Energy bookkeeping before the wires.</strong> The field stores energy — you can
    compute it as the work to assemble the charges — and the energy density is proportional
    to E². That idea, innocent here, becomes capacitors (the circuits chapter), the laser's
    energy store, and the vacuum's own strange bookkeeping in advanced field theory. It is
    also, quietly, why atoms and chemistry exist: the field between nucleus and electrons
    sets every orbital, and every chemical bond is an electrostatic negotiation. The deeper
    lesson of electrostatics is architectural: fields are not a diagram aid but
    physical objects with energy, momentum and their own dynamics — Maxwell's chapters
    ahead are the story of setting them moving.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Coulomb (1785); Millikan (1909); OpenStax, College Physics (ch. 18, Electric Charge and Electric Field); NOAA lightning safety guidance; NIST constants (e, k).</p>
